Example configurations for reading DHT11
========================================

The configuration should be placed in /etc/ws/sensors/dht11.json. It is a
JSON array with one object per sensor. Without the file, one sensor on GPIO 4
is read.

Basic reading of sensor
-----------------------

[
  {
    "pin": 4,
    "internal": false
  }
]


Indicating that the sensor is internal (to the enclosure)
---------------------------------------------------------

[
  {
    "pin": 4,
    "internal": true
  }
]


Declaring where the sensor is
-----------------------------

"location" is "{{node}}" for a sensor at the node's own position, "{{none}}"
for one that deliberately has none, or an object giving latitude and longitude
in decimal degrees, with altitude and accuracy in metres if surveyed. Omitted,
the reading's location is null.

[
  {
    "pin": 4,
    "internal": true,
    "location": "{{node}}"
  },
  {
    "pin": 17,
    "internal": false,
    "location": { "latitude": 51.4967, "longitude": -0.1764, "altitude": 12.0, "accuracy": 5.0 }
  }
]


Setting a custom sensor ID
--------------------------

If omitted, sensor_id is "<serial>_dht11", where <serial> is the Raspberry Pi
serial number. Where more than one entry omits it, each is instead
"<serial>_dht11_pin<N>", so that two sensors do not share an id. Each
measurement appends its own name, giving for example
"<serial>_dht11_temperature".

[
  {
    "pin": 4,
    "internal": true,
    "sensor_id": "My_Custom_ID"
  }
]

This produces the readings "My_Custom_ID_temperature" and
"My_Custom_ID_humidity".


Setting a custom sensor name
----------------------------

sensor_name is a human-readable label reported in the "sensor_name" field of
each reading. It does not change "sensor", which is always "dht11_temperature"
or "dht11_humidity", nor "sensor_id".

[
  {
    "pin": 4,
    "internal": true,
    "sensor_name": "Enclosure DHT11"
  }
]
